":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-08","committee":"Environment & Economy Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"Community & Housing Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-10","committee":"Corporate Resources Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Cabinet","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Clwyd Pension Fund Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-18","committee":"Education, Youth & Culture Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed; Special Meeting","webcast":false,"url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/ieListDocuments.aspx?CId=512&MId=6254"},{"date":"2026-09-22","committee":"Flintshire County Council","time":"10.30 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Education, Youth & Culture Overview & Scrutiny Committee","time":"2.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","time":"10.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Flintshire achieves \u00a30.79m underspend as social care pressures mount","lead":"Flintshire County Council's auditors gave an unqualified opinion on the 2025/26 accounts with no concerns about the council's ability to continue operating. The council underspent by \u00a30.790m overall, despite significant demand-led overspends: residential care for older people cost \u00a31.763m more than budgeted, homecare \u00a31.632m over, and out-of-county placements for people with learning disabilities \u00a32.573m over. The council offset these through underspends in fleet services and regulatory services, and pay award savings. It achieved 96% of its planned cost reductions. Council Fund reserves rose to \u00a317.257m, above the policy minimum of \u00a38.985m, boosted by an additional \u00a31.205m from Welsh Government funding that the council chose to add to reserves to build resilience against \"a challenging future funding outlook\". Welsh Government's funding increase of 3.8% fell below the Welsh average of 4.5%, and provided no guidance on allocations for 2027/28 onwards.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Enc. 1","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s96844/96844.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Flintshire's accounts cleared with no going concern doubts despite social care cost pressures","lead":"The council's auditor gave an unqualified opinion on its 2025/26 financial statements with no material uncertainties identified about its ability to continue as a going concern. The council underspent by \u00a30.790m overall despite significant demand-led overspends in social services: residential care for older people ran \u00a31.763m over budget, homecare \u00a31.632m over, and out-of-county placements for learning disability \u00a32.573m over. The council offset these through underspends elsewhere and 96% delivery of planned cost reductions in-year. Council Fund reserves increased to \u00a317.257m, above the policy minimum of \u00a38.985m, boosted by a \u00a31.205m addition from Welsh Government funding, which the council chose to add to reserves to build resilience. The council received a 3.8% funding increase in 2025/26, below the Welsh average of 4.5%, and Welsh Government has provided no guidance on revenue allocations for 2027/28 onwards, creating what the council describes as \"extremely challenging\" planning conditions.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Enc. 1","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s96844/96844.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Flintshire's auditor clears accounts despite sharp increase in social care overspends","lead":"Flintshire County Council received an unqualified audit opinion on its 2025/26 accounts, with the auditor identifying no material uncertainties about the council's ability to continue operating. The council underspent overall by \u00a30.790m despite significant demand-led pressures in social services: residential care for older people ran \u00a31.763m over budget, homecare \u00a31.632m over, and out-of-county placements for learning disability \u00a32.573m over. The council offset these through savings elsewhere, including a \u00a31.300m underspend in fleet services and pay award savings of \u00a31.036m, and achieved 96% of its planned cost reductions. Council Fund reserves rose to \u00a317.257m, above the policy minimum of \u00a38.985m, boosted by a \u00a31.205m contribution from Welsh Government's final settlement that the council added to reserves to build resilience. However, the council received a 3.8% funding increase in 2025/26, below the Welsh average of 4.5%, and Welsh Government has not announced revenue allocations for 2027/28 onwards, creating significant planning uncertainty.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Enc. 1","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s96844/96844.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","paper_title":"Enc. 2","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s96845/96845.pdf","headline":"Income misclassification found: \u00a3426,000 coded as capital grant instead of revenue.","detail":"One instance identified where revenue income of \u00a3426,000 was incorrectly classified as capital grant income. Review of the entire capital grant population confirmed this was isolated. No amendment to accounts required. Auditor recommended checks of income categorisation prior to account compilation. Management accepted and committed to reminding finance officers of correct treatment by 31 March 2027.","category":"audit","matters":"Income misclassification distorts the revenue versus capital split and can mask the council's true spending and financing position. While isolated, it indicates a control weakness in income coding procedures that should be monitored.","figures":"\u00a3426,000 misclassified; this was the only error identified in the entire capital grant population.","quote":"Our audit identified one instance where revenue income of \u00a3426,000 had been incorrectly classified as capital grant income. We reviewed the capital grant population, and it was confirmed this was the only error","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","paper_title":"Enc. 2","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s96845/96845.pdf","headline":"No material uncertainties identified relating to going concern.","detail":"The auditor has concluded that use of the going concern basis of accounting is appropriate. No material uncertainties have been identified relating to events or conditions that could cast significant doubt on the council's ability to continue as a going concern for at least 12 months from the financial statement authorisation date.","category":"governance","matters":"Going concern status underpins financial statement reliability and the council's ability to repay borrowing. Identification of material going concern issues would signal financial distress and potential covenant breach or default risk.","figures":"No quantitative thresholds; qualitative assessment based on audit procedures.","quote":"Based on the work I have performed, I have not identified any material uncertainties relating to events or conditions that, individually or collectively, may cast significant doubt on the Flintshire County Council and the group's ability to continue to adopt the going concern basis of accounting","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","paper_title":"Enc. 2","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s96845/96845.pdf","headline":"Unqualified audit opinion issued on 2025-26 accounts with no uncorrected misstatements.","detail":"An unqualified audit opinion will be issued confirming the financial statements give a true and fair view of the council's financial position as at 31 March 2026 and have been properly prepared. No uncorrected misstatements were identified. One corrected misstatement involved reclassification of Property, Plant and Equipment items between asset categories with no net impact on the summary statements.","category":"audit","matters":"An unqualified opinion confirms the reliability of the council's published financial statements used by lenders in creditworthiness assessment. Absence of uncorrected misstatements ensures the financial position is fairly stated.","figures":"One corrected reclassification: Other land and Buildings \u00a316.317m to \u00a35.578m; Assets Under Construction \u00a31.063m to \u00a311.802m; no net impact on accounts.","quote":"We intend to issue an unqualified opinion on the accounts","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","paper_title":"Enc. 2","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s96845/96845.pdf","headline":"Mutual Investment Model project for school construction properly accounted for with no audit issues.","detail":"The council has entered a Mutual Investment Model project for construction of a new primary and secondary school with associated infrastructure and maintenance. The auditor reviewed the full MIM contract, assessed asset recognition on the balance sheet, evaluated assumptions in valuing the asset and forecasting unitary charges, and verified disclosures of long-term commitments and financial obligations. No issues identified.","category":"capital","matters":"MIM arrangements create long-term financial obligations materially affecting cash flows and the council's future debt servicing capacity. Proper accounting and full disclosure of these commitments is essential for lenders assessing repayment ability.","figures":"No specific financial figures for the MIM project provided in this report.","quote":"The Council has entered a MIM project for the construction of a new primary and secondary school and associated infrastructure and maintenance.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Governance and Audit Committee","paper_title":"Enc. 2","paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s96845/96845.pdf","headline":"Triennial pension fund revaluation implemented for first time in 2025-26 accounts.","detail":"A triennial valuation of the Local Government Pension Scheme was undertaken as at 31 March 2025 and will impact disclosures in the council's 2025-26 financial statements for the first time. The audit team assessed the actuary's competence, tested data accuracy, and reviewed the reasonableness of assumptions. No issues were identified with the valuation.","category":"audit","matters":"Pension liabilities are material to the council's balance sheet. A triennial revaluation represents a material change to liability measurement that affects the reported financial position and covenant compliance ratios lenders monitor.","figures":"Triennial valuation dated 31 March 2025; specific pension liability quantum not disclosed in this report.","quote":"A triennial valuation of the scheme has been undertaken as at 31 March 2025, which will impact upon disclosures in the Council's financial statements for the first time in the 2025-26 financial year.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-24"},"name":"Flintshire","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-29","since_iso":"2026-08-30","distress":false,"headline":"Income misclassification found: \u00a3426,000 coded as capital grant instead of revenue.","lead":"Income misclassification distorts the revenue versus capital split and can mask the council's true spending and financing position. While isolated, it indicates a control weakness in income coding procedures that should be monitored.","also":["94 other findings in this window","81 papers caught"],"watch":"Environment & Economy Overview & Scrutiny Committee on 2026-10-06","n_findings":95,"n_documents":81,"top_paper_title":"Enc. 2","top_paper_url":"https://committeemeetings.flintshire.gov.uk/documents/s96845/96845.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":132,"news_drafts_total":3}}